WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.423
Disabling certificate validation is a dangerous anti-pattern, even when it begins as a local testing shortcut.

2
00:00:07.423 --> 00:00:14.170
Certificate validation is part of how the application confirms service identity and transport trust.

3
00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:18.084
Removing that check changes the meaning of the connection.

4
00:00:18.084 --> 00:00:29.960
Temporary trust-all callbacks, broad certificate exceptions, or environment flags can drift into production when they are copied, forgotten, or treated as harmless scaffolding.

5
00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:36.506
Development trust decisions should be scoped, documented, and separated from production behavior.

6
00:00:36.506 --> 00:00:40.015
Outbound HTTP clients need deliberate configuration.

7
00:00:40.015 --> 00:00:51.756
Lifetimes, timeouts, cancellation, retry behavior, base addresses, default headers, credentials, proxy settings, and logging rules all affect reliability and confidentiality.

8
00:00:51.756 --> 00:00:56.884
External calls are part of the application boundary, not plumbing to ignore.

9
00:00:56.884 --> 00:01:04.037
Creating and disposing HttpClient instances casually can create connection and socket-management problems.

10
00:01:04.037 --> 00:01:15.441
Reusing clients incorrectly can also cause problems, such as leaking headers, cookies, bearer values, tenant context, or base address assumptions across unrelated calls.

11
00:01:15.441 --> 00:01:21.244
The safer habit is to use a reviewed client factory or service-specific client design.

12
00:01:21.244 --> 00:01:26.103
Retries and fan-out should not amplify load against downstream services.

13
00:01:26.103 --> 00:01:32.918
A retry policy that looks reasonable in isolation can become harmful when many requests fail at once.

14
00:01:32.918 --> 00:01:41.150
Bound concurrency, add cancellation, apply timeouts, and make retry behavior appropriate for the dependency and operation.

15
00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:52.217
External service errors should be handled predictably without exposing secrets, tokens, URLs with credentials, raw sensitive payloads, or internal provider details.

16
00:01:52.217 --> 00:02:01.529
Logs should capture safe context: dependency name, operation category, status category, correlation ID, latency, retry count, and outcome.
